hposner1@swarthmore.edu (Howard) writes:
> Though an experienced gamer, I am quite new to NetHack. 
>   I am unsure as to how one handles fountains in the game. 
>   I read an article by Dylan O'Donnell that gives a variety of
> possibilities regarding what might occur when one drinks from a
> fountain or dips an item into a fountain.
>  Even so, I still am not sure what to do when I find a fountain or a
> sink, for that matter.

My own practice:

* Never quaff from a fountain. I know that I'm passing up potentially
  useful magic fountains by this, but I just don't want to have to
  deal with the hassle of either getting my stuff cursed or leaving it
  somewhere safe to avoid it.

* If I'm an Excalibur candidate, dip repeatedly for it as soon as
  possible. The odds are good enough that the reward is worth the
  risk.

* Otherwise, only dip into fountains (blanking stuff, generally) when
  I feel confident that I will be able to take on the most probable
  bad outcomes; a water demon who whistles up Yeenoghu, a stream of
  moccasins, a pesky nymph. In practice, this means around the time
  that I'm thinking of levelling up for the Quest or of taking on
  Medusa.

  (Yes, I know creating pools is a generally safer thing to do. It's
  also more trouble than I consider it worth.)

* Never quaff from sinks. I could probably safely do so once I have
  polymorph control, but by that point there's no real reason for it.

* Kick sinks when I feel happy about taking on a black pudding;
  namely, when all of my gear is either rustproof, non-rustprone, or
  disposable. It's generally worth getting the ring out of each sink,
  and puddings are handy for intrinsics; it'll depend on the character
  whether the dishwasher is helpful or a nuisance, but either way
  isn't a great factor in my consideration.

These methods work for me. I'm certain others have others.

